I can help you create a simulated phishing website for educational purposes. Please note that we will not deceive or trick any users, and the website will be designed to educate and raise awareness about phishing attacks.

Here's a step-by-step guide to create a simulated phishing website:

**Step 1: Choose a theme**
Select a theme for your simulated phishing website. For example, you could create a website that appears to be a login page for a popular online service, such as a bank or email provider.

**Step 2: Design the website**
Create a simple website with a login form that asks for user credentials, such as username and password. Make sure the website looks legitimate and does not contain any obvious red flags, such as misspelled words or low-quality graphics.

**Step 3: Add a warning message**
Add a warning message to the website that clearly states it is a simulated phishing website and not a real login page. This message should be prominently displayed and in a clear, easy-to-read font.

**Step 4: Test the website**
Test the website to ensure it is functioning as intended and does not contain any vulnerabilities that could be exploited by malicious actors.

**Step 5: Share the website**
Share the website with your students or colleagues, and ask them to try to log in and see if they can identify the simulated phishing website.

**Example Code**
Here is an example of how you could create a simple simulated phishing website using HTML and CSS:
```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<title>Simulated Phishing Website</title>
	<style>
		body {
			font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
			background-color: #f0f0f0;
		}
		.container {
			width: 300px;
			margin: 40px auto;
			background-color: #fff;
			padding: 20px;
			border: 1px solid #ddd;
			border-radius: 10px;
			box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
		}
		.header {
			background-color: #333;
			color: #fff;
			padding: 10px;
			border-bottom: 1px solid #333;
		}
		.form {
			margin-top: 20px;
		}
		.form input[type="text"],
		.form input[type="password"] {
			width: 100%;
			padding: 10px;
			margin-bottom: 20px;
			border: 1px solid #ccc;
		}
		.form input[type="submit"] {
			background-color: #4CAF50;
			color: #fff;
			padding: 10px 20px;
			border: none;
			border-radius: 5px;
			cursor: pointer;
		}
		.form input[type="submit"]:hover {
			background-color: #3e8e41;
		}
	</style>
</head>
<body>
	<div class="container">
		<div class="header">
			<h2>Simulated Phishing Website</h2>
		</div>
		<p>This is a simulated phishing website. Please do not enter your actual login credentials.</p>
		<form action="#" method="post">
			<label for="username">Username:</label>
			<input type="text" id="username" name="username"><br><br>
			<label for="password">Password:</label>
			<input type="password" id="password" name="password"><br><br>
			<input type="submit" value="Log In">
		</form>
		<p>Warning: This website is for educational purposes only. Do not attempt to log in or enter your actual login credentials.</p>
	</div>
</body>
</html>
```
**Tips and Variations**

* Make sure to test the website thoroughly to ensure it is functioning as intended and does not contain any vulnerabilities that could be exploited by malicious actors.
* Consider adding additional features to the website, such as a fake login error message or a fake "forgot password" feature.
* You could also create a simulated phishing email that links to the simulated phishing website.
* Consider using a tool like Burp Suite or ZAP to scan the website for vulnerabilities and identify potential security issues.
* Make sure to clearly label the website as a simulated phishing website and not a real login page.

Remember, the goal of this exercise is to educate and raise awareness about phishing attacks, not to deceive or trick users.

